From: PowerVLC <powervlc@localhost>
Date: Sat, 18 Jul 2026 18:45:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] read_cache: page-align chunk buffers and pre-fault them (Tiger raw-device physio bug)

Mac OS X 10.4 (xnu-792) physio silently short-transfers >MAXPHYS reads
from raw disk devices into non-page-aligned, unfaulted user buffers
while returning the full requested size; the untransferred tail reads
back as zero-fill pages. Mounted DVD images (data path = /dev/rdiskN)
hit this as soon as the read-ahead exceeds 63 blocks: zeroed sectors,
'Expected NAV packet' and a dead input when entering a title. Align
the cache chunks to the 4 KiB page size and touch every page after
(re)allocation. Verified on a PowerPC G4 Mac mini.
---
--- a/src/read_cache.c	2026-07-18 18:23:09
+++ b/src/read_cache.c	2026-07-18 18:40:44
@@ -43,9 +43,32 @@
 
 #define READ_CACHE_CHUNKS 10
 
-/* all cache chunks must be memory aligned to allow use of raw devices */
-#define ALIGNMENT 2048
+/* All cache chunks must be memory aligned to allow use of raw devices.
+ * PowerVLC: PAGE-align them (4096) instead of merely 2048: the Mac OS X
+ * 10.4 kernel (xnu-792) physio path silently SHORT-TRANSFERS reads
+ * larger than MAXPHYS (128 KiB) from a raw disk device when the user
+ * buffer is not page-aligned and its pages are not yet faulted in --
+ * read() still returns the full requested size, but only the first
+ * (MAXPHYS - misalignment) bytes are filled; the remaining pages read
+ * back as untouched zero-fill. With 2048-alignment the chunk buffer
+ * ends up page+2048, so every read-ahead beyond 63 blocks returned a
+ * zero tail ("demux error! 00 00 00", dead input on mounted DVD
+ * images, whose data path is the raw /dev/rdiskN device). Verified on
+ * a G4 Mini: page-aligned buffers transfer fully even when unfaulted.
+ * The buffers are also touched once after (re)allocation (see
+ * PRE_FAULT below) as belt-and-braces for the unfaulted half of the
+ * kernel trigger. */
+#define ALIGNMENT 4096
 
+/* Touch every page of a freshly (re)allocated cache buffer so the
+ * pages are resident before the first raw-device read into them. */
+#define PRE_FAULT( p, size ) \
+  do { \
+    size_t off_; \
+    for( off_ = 0; off_ < (size); off_ += 4096 ) \
+      ((volatile uint8_t *)(p))[off_] = 0; \
+  } while( 0 )
+
 #define READ_AHEAD_SIZE_MIN 4
 #define READ_AHEAD_SIZE_MAX 512
 
@@ -186,6 +209,8 @@
         (uint8_t *)(((uintptr_t)self->chunk[use].cache_buffer_base & ~((uintptr_t)(ALIGNMENT - 1))) + ALIGNMENT);
       dprintf("pre_cache DVD read realloc happened\n");
       self->chunk[use].cache_malloc_size = block_count;
+      PRE_FAULT( self->chunk[use].cache_buffer,
+                 block_count * DVD_VIDEO_LB_LEN );
     } else {
       /* we still haven't found a cache chunk, let's allocate a new one */
       for (i = 0; i < READ_CACHE_CHUNKS; i++)
@@ -205,6 +230,8 @@
         self->chunk[i].cache_malloc_size = block_count > 500 ? block_count : 500;
         dprintf("pre_cache DVD read malloc %d blocks\n",
           (block_count > 500 ? block_count : 500 ));
+        PRE_FAULT( self->chunk[i].cache_buffer,
+                   self->chunk[i].cache_malloc_size * DVD_VIDEO_LB_LEN );
       }
     }
   }
